### First-party Adea palettes

Slate and High Contrast retain the semantic and ANSI values from Adea's existing product theme
definitions. Their records identify Adea and Apache-2.0; this package makes no upstream or
third-party source claim for those ANSI constants. The exact retained ANSI values are asserted in
`tests/provenance.test.ts`.

### Adea's two defaults are composed from three of these

Neither default is a copy of a published palette and neither is hand-authored. Each is
built in the vendor step from two donors, named in its `palettes/*.json` under
`source.composedFrom`:

| | canvas | canvas hue | hues |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Adea Dark** | Aardvark Ink `#0f141f` | 265° | GitHub Dark Default |
| **Adea Light** | Nord Light `#e3e9f4` | 262° | GitHub Dark Default |

Both take their hues from the same palette, so a red is the same red and a blue the same
blue in each; only their canvases come from different projects, each chosen for the
canvas rather than for its colours. The composition exists because those two properties
are in tension inside a single palette: one tuned for a comfortable canvas mutes its hues
to agree with it, and one tuned for vivid syntax colours tends to sit on a canvas that is
very dark or very flat.

All three donors are MIT-licensed through the same iTerm2-Color-Schemes chain as the rest
of the catalogue, so the composed results are used under MIT as well as under this
package's Apache-2.0.

**Neither canvas was adjusted to make the composition work, and no hue was either.**
GitHub's hues measure between 7.2:1 and 8.8:1 against Aardvark Ink's canvas — *more*
contrast than they have against GitHub's own — so the dark theme's vibrant set arrives
intact. On Nord Light's canvas the same hues measure between 2.5:1 and 4.1:1, which is
legible as an accent and not as terminal text, so the whole set is **transposed down by
one common step of 0.244 in lightness**; hue and chroma are untouched, and the relative
brightness ordering that makes an ANSI palette an ANSI palette is preserved. Both facts
are asserted rather than assumed.

One value *is* a deliberate deviation, and it is aesthetic rather than a correction:
Nord Light's canvas carries 0.010 of chroma against Aardvark Ink's 0.024, so rendering
the same hue at half the tint made the light theme read as neutral grey beside a partner
that is plainly navy. The light canvas is deepened to 0.016 — two thirds of the dark
theme's — at the same lightness and hue, so no contrast pairing moves. It is recorded in
`COMPOSED_SOURCES` and can be reverted by deleting one line.

### Two entries that need a note

**Gruvbox** grants the MIT/X11 licence in the *Licence* section of its README and
ships no `LICENSE` file, which is why automated licence detection reports nothing
for the repository. The README is the grant, and it is recorded here as MIT. The
source for this statement is
https://github.com/morhetz/gruvbox#license.

**Monokai** is the one family whose own repository carries no licence at all: the
repository under the Monokai organisation has none, so there is no permissively
licensed artefact of Monokai's own to vendor from. The values in this package are
therefore taken from the MIT-licensed iTerm2-Color-Schemes port through the chain
above, which is the artefact the MIT licence attaches to, and Monokai is credited as
the design's origin without a licence claim being made on its behalf. If Monokai's
terms are ever clarified, this entry should be revisited — and if they turn out to
be incompatible, `monokai` should be removed from `VENDORED_SOURCES` and the
catalogue regenerated, which is a one-line change plus a rebuild.

### One deliberate deviation from the vendored values

**One Dark** is normalized from Atom's own editor palette rather than from the
terminal port. iTerm2's One Dark port darkens the canvas to `#21252b` so that a
terminal window reads as a distinct surface, but "One Dark" as a design means Atom's
`hsl(220, 13%, 18%)` = `#282c34`, with comments at `#5c6370` rather than the port's
`#767676`. Adea themes an application, so the project's value is the one used. The
substitution is declared in `src/sources.ts` and reported as a finding on every
build.

